.TH SDBCTL 1 "2026-11-15" "sdbctl 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sdbctl \- inspect and control systemd-boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sdbctl
.RI [ verb ]
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sdbctl
shows what systemd-boot will boot, lets you boot another entry for the next
restart, and explains why an entry is missing from the boot menu.
.PP
It is CLI-first: every operation is a verb with machine-readable
.B \-\-json
output, so the same commands work in a shell, in a script, and from an agent.
The full-screen editor is available as
.B sdbctl edit
for work that benefits from a UI.
.PP
Most operations write to the EFI System Partition and need root. Every write
or delete is snapshotted first, so changes can be rolled back with
.BR "sdbctl undo" .
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B (no verb), status
Boot status: the loader binaries found, the default entry, the pending
one-shot, and every entry with its warnings.
.TP
.B list
One line per boot entry. With
.B \-\-ids
only the identifiers are printed, one per line, which is what completion
scripts and shell loops want. With
.B \-\-json
the full listing is printed as JSON.
.TP
.BI "boot " entry
Boot
.I entry
exactly once on the next restart, by writing the
.B LoaderEntryOneShot
EFI variable. With
.B \-\-reboot
the machine restarts immediately as well.
.TP
.BI "default " entry
Make
.I entry
the persistent default in
.IR loader.conf .
The special value
.B @saved
remembers the last manually chosen entry.
.TP
.BI "timeout " value
Set the boot menu timeout: a number of seconds, or
.BR menu\-force ,
.BR menu\-hidden ,
.BR menu\-disabled .
.TP
.BI "set " "key value"
Set any
.I loader.conf
option. An empty value clears the option, removing its line. Unknown keys are
refused rather than guessed at.
.TP
.BI "rm " entry
Delete a type #1 boot entry. Unified kernel images are refused: they belong to
the package manager.
.TP
.B undo
Restore the most recent snapshot.
.TP
.B snapshots
List the snapshots taken before changes.
.TP
.B check
Full diagnostics. Exits with status 1 when problems were found, which makes
it usable as a gate in scripts and CI.
.TP
.B edit
Launch the full-screen editor.
.TP
.B mcp
Serve the same operations over stdio as a Model Context Protocol server. See
.B "MCP SERVER"
below.
.TP
.BI "completion " shell
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.B zsh
or
.BR fish .
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.TP
.B help
Print a usage summary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-esp " path"
Boot directory to operate on. The default is the first of
.IR /boot ,
.I /boot/efi ,
.I /efi
that contains a
.I loader
directory, which covers both plain ESP layouts and XBOOTLDR setups.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable JSON. The schema is stable and identical to what the
MCP server returns.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would change without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Act on an entry systemd-boot would hide.
.TP
.BI \-\-yes " / " \-y
Skip confirmation prompts.
.TP
.BI \-\-quiet " / " \-q
Suppress explanatory prose.
.SH MCP SERVER
.B sdbctl mcp
speaks the Model Context Protocol over stdio. It is read-only unless started
with
.BR \-\-allow\-write ,
in which case the mutating tools
.BR sdbctl_boot ,
.BR sdbctl_set_default ,
.BR sdbctl_set_timeout ,
.BR sdbctl_set_key ,
.B sdbctl_remove_entry
and
.B sdbctl_undo
are advertised as well. Rebooting additionally requires an explicit
.B reboot
argument.
.PP
Every mutating result reports the snapshot identifier and the undo command, so
a client can reverse what it just did.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SDBCTL_EFIVARS
Overrides the efivarfs mount point, which is
.I /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
by default. Useful in chroots and containers where
.I /sys
is not bind-mounted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An operation failed, or
.B check
found problems.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /boot/loader/loader.conf ", " /boot/efi/loader/loader.conf ", " /efi/loader/loader.conf
The boot loader configuration.
.TP
.IR /boot/loader/entries/*.conf
Boot entry descriptions.
.TP
.IR /boot/loader/sdbctl-backups/
Snapshots taken before changes.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the status, and only the entries that will not boot:
.PP
.EX
sdbctl status
sdbctl list --json | jq -r '.entries[] | select(.hidden) | .fullId'
.EE
.PP
Boot another distribution once and reboot into it:
.PP
.EX
sudo sdbctl boot fedora.conf --reboot
.EE
.PP
Make it the permanent default, and remember the last choice instead:
.PP
.EX
sudo sdbctl default fedora.conf
sudo sdbctl default @saved
.EE
.PP
Use it as a gate in a provisioning script:
.PP
.EX
sdbctl check || exit 1
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bootctl (1),
.BR systemd-boot (7),
.BR loader.conf (5)
.PP
The Boot Loader Specification:
.UR https://uapi-group.org/specifications/specs/boot_loader_specification/
.UE
